/// MAALIK OS push registration (Expo push token) for a user's phone.
model PushToken {
  id         String   @id @default(cuid())
  userId     String
  user       User     @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  token      String   @unique
  deviceName String?
  /// MAALIK OS 1.0.14+: rings Urgent alerts like a call, so Urgent pushes to
  /// this phone go as data only (the app shows them itself). 6 Oct 2026.
  fullScreen Boolean  @default(false)
  /// NOBODY IS SIGNED IN ON THIS PHONE RIGHT NOW (F-05, 10 Oct 2026).
  ///
  /// Signing out of MAALIK OS used to leave this row exactly as it was, and
  /// Expo does not know or care whether an app has a session - so an Urgent
  /// alert kept arriving on a phone nobody was signed into, title and body
  /// and all. On a lock screen that reads "Approval waiting - invoice
  /// INV-BR001-2026-0042, Rs 4,38,000, Sharma Traders", to whoever is holding
  /// the phone. That is the security question that kept logged-out delivery
  /// open since 6 Oct.
  ///
  /// The answer is not to stop delivering - being reached is the whole point
  /// of an Urgent alert - but to deliver NOTHING but the knock. The phone
  /// still rings full-screen; the card says only that something urgent is
  /// waiting and to sign in. Nothing about the business reaches a locked-out
  /// device, and `server/notify` is where that is enforced.
  signedOut  Boolean  @default(false)
  lastSeenAt DateTime @default(now())
  createdAt  DateTime @default(now())

  @@index([userId])
}

/// Per-business switches for field jobs (workflow spec §5) - lib/job-settings
/// parses `settings` and supplies defaults for anything missing.
/// How this branch works out a sales incentive (workstream H, 1 Oct 2026).
///
/// Before this there was no rule anywhere: `Quotation.incentivePaise` was a
/// number somebody typed on each deal. The scheme - percentage of value, of
/// margin, slabs, per product, or still a manual figure - is a BUSINESS
/// choice, so it is configuration rather than code.
///
/// One JSON column rather than a column per scheme: the shape differs per
/// basis (slabs are a list, a percentage is a number), and `parseIncentiveRule`
/// in lib validates it on the way in and out. Same pattern as JobSettings.
/// How a branch sends email and WhatsApp (workstream D, 1 Oct 2026).
///
/// One row per branch per channel. `credentials` holds the provider's fields,
/// with anything the provider marks `secret` ENCRYPTED by server/secret-box
/// before it is written - never plaintext.
///
/// This is the deliberate exception to the F2.5 rule that a credential never
/// reaches the database. The biometric connector runs on the client's own PC
/// and can keep its password there; an email provider is called from our
/// server on the client's behalf, and a Vercel environment variable is
/// per-deployment rather than per-tenant, so one client's key would serve
/// every client. The secret-box module argues this at length.
